  "notes": "The era's most reliable attitude-anthem template: 92–140 straight with the backbeat on 2&4 and the dance-facing singles clustering tightly at 122–129, which is where `bpm.mode` sits. Three named tonics (B minor, A and G major) put `keys` in the model. The drum block reads the entry's own explanation of why the kit sounds wrong on purpose — the 2001 single's producer \"programmed the drums, played the bass and added horn samples, deliberately choosing 'every wrong instrument'\" — so the deltas are \"kick 1 and 3 plus an 8th pickup\" (second anchor \"4&\"), \"big snare on 2&4\" (a high accent tier), and \"16th hat\", with the horn samples living in the perc block as a `perc` lane and the rock-era \"marching 8th-snare pre-chorus\" as a snare-roll ladder. Harmony is i–VI–III–VII for the minor anthems and I–V–vi–IV for the major ones, with \"a suspended pre-chorus that hangs on IV before dropping to the tonic\" — hence the sus substitution and the IV–i family. The bass is \"played bass or a fat mono synth bass on roots in 8ths\", and on the new-wave-styled singles \"a synth-bass riff as the hook itself\", which is why `followRootsProb` sits at 0.72 rather than up with the root-lockers. The melody \"sits low and raspy in the verse then jumps an octave for a shouted chorus\" — a 0.26 `octaveJumpProb`, the highest in this batch. ⚠ `confidence` is medium because the research rates it high-medium. ⚠ The **INFERRED** parameters are all drum probabilities.",
